\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:vfour-9}

\subsection{Service recovery and coordinated plan changes}

The network results distinguish restoring service from choosing how to restore it. Every admissible model-generated plan attains the optimal urgent and total service counts, while most remaining quality gaps concern changes to the incumbent plan. Once service is secured, repair quality depends on coordinating departure shifts and charging decisions across vehicle chains. The minimum-change results identify instances where this coordination extends beyond the evaluated local neighborhood.

Shared facilities connect these decisions across UAVs. In M01-B, sufficient charging for downstream service becomes compatible with the network only when another UAV releases the charger earlier. The Core capacity counterfactuals show a related effect: a resource restriction can raise the minimum repair cost without changing a designated pairwise contrast. Assessing the full operation is therefore necessary to explain the value of a local adjustment.

\subsection{Physical timing and operating priorities}

The common flight phases connect the timing of each mission to its battery consumption. Resource reservations then determine when physically feasible flights and charges can be scheduled together. Finer booking windows improve some reference schedules while preserving their service counts and flight energy. This result identifies facility resolution as a source of scheduling differences within the same physical model.

Recovery priorities determine which of the feasible alternatives is preferred. The Core preference analysis exposes a trade-off between preserving the incumbent plan and reducing tardiness, as well as a recurring exchange between charging changes and departure shifts. Interpreting a reference-quality gap alongside its mission events shows whether it reflects avoidable coordination loss or an alternative that becomes preferable under a different operating objective.

\subsection{Using complementary repair candidates}

Generated plans complement local optimization when they coordinate decisions that the returned local solution leaves unresolved. Deterministic candidates also improve some admissible model plans and provide alternatives when generation fails to yield an executable response. Verified selection retains these contributions through a common feasibility check and objective comparison.

The benefit depends on the task and comparator. Expanding the local neighborhood closes the model-assisted quality gains on Core-72, while complementary coverage remains on the network tasks. The results support using generated plans as an additional source of coordinated repairs, with their value established against explicit constraints, operating priorities and optimization baselines.

