\subsection{Operating priorities and physical margins}
The objective hierarchy strongly influences the recovery action. Prioritizing stability produces mostly waiting and departure changes on the original networks; prioritizing tardiness yields more rerouting and more on-time urgent service. The latter improvement requires only a small aggregate energy increase on tasks serving the same missions, although its distribution across chains changes charging and resource access. In contrast, lengthening a closure can make rerouting and charging necessary for service even under the original stability preference.

