\subsection{The role of generated alternatives}
Generated candidates improve coverage relative to a single fixed neighborhood and retain some complementarity with iterative or enlarged local search. Full-MILP, however, closes all quality gaps on the evaluated networks, and the historical response comparison supplies no general timing advantage for generation. The useful distinction is between proposing a coordinated alternative and outperforming the strongest available optimization method.

Verified selection provides a common way to use available candidates under the stated constraints and objective. Its benefit depends on what the constituent methods have delivered by the decision time. At the evaluated scales, complete optimization is an effective primary source of repairs; generated alternatives can supplement a deliberately restricted search or provide a different feasible plan. Their role in larger or differently structured operations requires evidence from those operations.

