\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:vfour-11}

This study examines generative plan repair for disrupted UAV logistics through ARCO-Bench. Optimized references and mission-event reconstruction connect the quality of generated plans to service retention, battery feasibility and shared-resource use. The network experiments identify coordinated repairs beyond the evaluated local neighborhood and show how charging decisions affect downstream service and access by other UAVs. Core counterfactuals and preference sensitivity explain why the preferred repair changes with resource capacity and operating priorities. Verified selection improves reference-quality coverage by retaining complementary model-generated and deterministic plans. The findings establish where generated alternatives add value to logistics recovery and which operational dependencies determine that value.

